            },
            "formats": [
              "heading"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "### Two Classic High School Examples (Must-Know for Exams!)",
            "start": 2397,
            "end": 2456
          },
          {
            "id": "p28",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "You will usually see two specific processes on your exams:",
            "start": 2458,
            "end": 2516
          },
          {
            "id": "p29",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "heading"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "#### 1. Mond’s Process (Used to purify **Nickel**)",
            "start": 2518,
            "end": 2568
          },
          {
            "id": "p30",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "list",
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "*   **The Trick:** Nickel reacts with **Carbon Monoxide ($\\text{CO}$)** gas.",
            "start": 2569,
            "end": 2645
          },
          {
            "id": "p31",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "list",
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "*   **Step 1:** Impure Nickel is heated with $\\text{CO}$ at about $50^\\circ\\text{C} - 70^\\circ\\text{C}$ ($330 - 350\\text{ K}$). It forms a gas called *Nickel tetracarbonyl*. Impurities are left behind.",
            "start": 2646,
            "end": 2847
          },
          {
            "id": "p32",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "equation"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "    $$\\text{Ni} + 4\\text{CO} \\rightarrow \\text{Ni(CO)}_4 \\text{ (Gas)}$$",
            "start": 2848,
            "end": 2920
          },
          {
            "id": "p33",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "list",
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "*   **Step 2:** This gas is then heated to a higher temperature ($200^\\circ\\text{C}$ or $\\approx 450 - 470\\text{ K}$). It breaks apart, giving pure solid Nickel and releasing the $\\text{CO}$ gas.",
            "start": 2921,
            "end": 3116
          },
          {
            "id": "p34",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "equation"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "    $$\\text{Ni(CO)}_4 \\rightarrow \\text{Ni (Pure Solid)} + 4\\text{CO}$$",
            "start": 3117,
            "end": 3188
          }
        ],
        "accuracy": {
          "verdict": "accurate",
          "reason": "The reaction equations, reagent (CO), formation of nickel tetracarbonyl, and the approximate temperature ranges (330-350 K and 450-470 K) are fully consistent with standard metallurgical chemistry.",
          "errors": []
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "u6",
        "kind": "EXAMPLE",
        "label": "Van Arkel-de Boer Method for Zirconium and Titanium",
        "attributes": {
          "context": "real_world",
          "treatment": "illustrative"
        },
        "subtopic_ids": [
          "s4"
        ],
        "rationale": "Illustrates vapour phase refining using the Van Arkel-de Boer method for purifying zirconium/titanium via iodide formation and tungsten wire decomposition.",
        "review_flags": [],
        "contextualization": {
          "value": "none",
          "evidence": []
        },
        "passages": [
          {
            "id": "p35",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "heading"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "#### 2. Van Arkel-de Boer Method (Used for **Zirconium** or **Titanium**)",
            "start": 3190,
            "end": 3263
          },
          {
            "id": "p36",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "list",
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "*   **The Trick:** These metals react with **Iodine ($\\text{I}_2$)**. This is used for metals used in the aerospace industry where even tiny impurities cause brittleness.",
            "start": 3264,
            "end": 3434
          },
          {
            "id": "p37",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "list",
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "*   **Step 1:** Impure Titanium or Zirconium is heated with Iodine in a vacuum to form a volatile iodide gas.",
            "start": 3435,
            "end": 3544
          },
          {
            "id": "p38",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "equation"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "    $$\\text{Zr} + 2\\text{I}_2 \\rightarrow \\text{ZrI}_4 \\text{ (Gas)}$$",
            "start": 3545,
            "end": 3615
          },
          {
            "id": "p39",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "list",
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "*   **Step 2:** The gas is passed over an extremely hot **tungsten filament** (around $1800\\text{ K}$). The intense heat breaks the gas apart. Pure metal sticks to the wire, and iodine vapour is recycled.",
            "start": 3616,
            "end": 3820
          },
          {
            "id": "p40",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "equation"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "    $$\\text{ZrI}_4 \\xrightarrow{\\text{Hot Tungsten Wire}} \\text{Zr (Pure Solid)} + 2\\text{I}_2$$",
            "start": 3821,
            "end": 3917
          }
        ],
        "accuracy": {
          "verdict": "accurate",
          "reason": "The chemical reactions, reagents, volatile iodide formation, and decomposition over an electrically heated tungsten filament around 1800 K accurately describe the Van Arkel process.",
          "errors": []
        }
      },
